@@ -142,6 +142,68 @@ ros2 launch bxi_example_py_elf3 example_launch_demo_hw.py
 
 This starts the robot hardware and the control policy.
 
+#### Head-only hardware bench test
+
+Do not use the full-body hardware launch when only testing the head. The robot
+must be lying stably or secured by a rated support; never leave it standing
+unsupported, and keep the emergency stop within reach. After sourcing the
+environment as `root`, run:
+
+```bash
+ros2 launch bxi_example_py_elf3 example_head_only_hw.launch.py
+```
+
+This dedicated launch fixes `motor_disable=0x1FFFFFFF`, disabling the 29 body
+motors while enabling only `head_z_joint` and `head_y_joint`. It does not start
+cameras and ignores `motion_commands` so the state machine cannot leave
+`zero_torque`. The startup log must contain:
+
+```text
+mode=HEAD_ONLY ... motor_disable=0x1FFFFFFF ... body_motors=disabled, head_motors=enabled
+```
+
+Stop immediately and do not send a head command if another mask is printed.
+The normal two-stage hardware reset still runs and may move the head to zero,
+so clear the head's motion envelope before starting.
+
+#### Head test while full-body control remains active
+
+Never switch a standing robot to the head-only launch: doing so disables all
+29 body motors and the robot will fall. A standing test requires deploying
+this controller while the robot is safely landed or supported, then restarting
+the full hardware chain with `enable_head:=true`. The running hardware node
+must be `hardware_elf3_head`, and the sole subscriber on
+`hardware/actuators_cmds_override` must be `bxi_example_py_elf3_demo`. If an
+older controller has no override topic, never compete with it by publishing
+a second stream directly to `hardware/actuators_cmds`.
+
+`head_control.py --hardware --standing-full-body` additionally verifies the
+number and ownership of every remote, state-machine, feedback, primary-command,
+and override ROS endpoint. It releases the head override if any endpoint
+disappears or conflicts. This mode does not replace a rated support,
+accessible emergency stop, or an on-site operator.
+
+After building and sourcing the robot workspace, start with conservative
+limits:
+
+```bash
+ros2 run bxi_example_py_elf3 elf3_head_control \
+  --hardware --standing-full-body \
+  --step-deg 2 --max-speed-deg 10 \
+  --yaw-limit-deg 15 --pitch-limit-deg 10 --duration 30
+```
+
+The controller reserves this override interface for `head_z_joint` and
+`head_y_joint` in both head-only and full-body modes. It rejects body, arm, and
+wrist joints, bounds head position and gains, and forbids velocity or torque
+feed-forward. A dexterous hand must keep using its independent driver
+interface. If a grasp controller also needs to take over arm joints, compose
+those commands inside the sole full-body controller; never add a second
+publisher to `hardware/actuators_cmds`. The default remote configuration starts
+the full hardware chain with `enable_head:=true`, so after this version has
+been deployed and restarted once while safely supported, the head controller
+can subsequently be started and stopped while the robot remains standing.
+
 ### Tips for Running a Control Program
 
 1. Control commands in the topic must be sent in the specified joint order. See the examples in `src/bxi_example_py_elf3` for the joint order.
